**Core idea.** This is **bulk-synchronous parallel**: a barrier at the end of each phase. In Java, use `CyclicBarrier`, `Phaser`, or a fresh `CountDownLatch` per phase.

**Case 1: W workers, one task each (T = W).** Each worker runs its phases in a loop and calls `barrier.await()` after each:

```java
CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(T);
for (int t = 0; t < T; t++) {
  int id = t;
  pool.submit(() -> {
    for (int p = 0; p < P; p++) {
      doPhase(id, p);
      barrier.await();   // waits until all T tasks finish phase p
    }
    return null;
  });
}
```

The barrier is *cyclic*, so it resets after every phase. Do not use a plain `CountDownLatch` here because it cannot be reused.

**Case 2: T greater than W (more tasks than workers).** Blocking inside a fixed pool deadlocks: workers wait at the barrier while the unstarted tasks have no thread. Fix by restructuring around phases, so the **driver** loops over phases and for each one submits all T tasks and waits for completion:

```java
for (int p = 0; p < P; p++) {
  CountDownLatch done = new CountDownLatch(T);   // or invokeAll
  for (int t = 0; t < T; t++) {
    int id = t, ph = p;
    pool.submit(() -> { try { doPhase(id, ph); } finally { done.countDown(); } });
  }
  done.await();       // barrier between phases
}
```

`ExecutorService.invokeAll` does the same. A `Phaser` supports a dynamic number of parties and per-phase actions, and `CyclicBarrier` accepts a barrier action that runs once per phase.

**Details to mention.** Always count down in a `finally` so a failing task cannot hang the barrier; handle `BrokenBarrierException` and interruption; stragglers slow every phase, so balance work or split tasks smaller; decide whether one failure cancels the run. Visibility is guaranteed because actions before a barrier happen-before actions after it.
